          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The blind responses identify belief primarily as personal trust and reliance on God or Christ for salvation, beyond bare intellectual assent. They also recognize obedient loyalty, faithfulness, or allegiance as a legitimate but less dominant emphasis.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book defines belief more narrowly as an obedient response to recognized truth, stressing the lexical and contextual relationship between believing and obeying rather than mere assent.\",\"rationale\":\"The entry does not introduce an unrecognized meaning or directly reject the consensus. Its obedience emphasis appears explicitly among the consensus alternatives and is compatible with the common claim that saving belief includes commitment rather than bare assent. It is nevertheless materially sharper than the consensus because it makes obedience central to the definition rather than a consequence or component of trust.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Contrasting belief with apeitheō and paralleling believing with obeying establish a close relationship, but do not by themselves prove that belief simply means obedience in every context; personal trust may remain the primary semantic idea, with obedience as its embodied expression.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
